406
00:21:56,941 --> 00:22:01,245
[SPEAKER_03]: So I got in, I stayed in the papers for a long time.

407
00:22:02,707 --> 00:22:06,030
[SPEAKER_03]: Even when I was over here in Tampa, we lived in Tampa for quite a while.

408
00:22:07,204 --> 00:22:10,406
[SPEAKER_03]: And the sport writer over there used to talk about him.

409
00:22:11,346 --> 00:22:17,229
[SPEAKER_03]: And Ruth, he was a funny guy, used to call me cousin.

410
00:22:18,749 --> 00:22:21,951
[SPEAKER_03]: Hey, cousin.

411
00:22:24,292 --> 00:22:26,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Which part did you like to pitch in best?

412
00:22:26,073 --> 00:22:26,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you have any favorites?

413
00:22:32,687 --> 00:22:35,668
[SPEAKER_03]: Some of them were short.

414
00:22:36,108 --> 00:22:39,268
[SPEAKER_03]: St. Louis, I spent three years in St. Louis.

415
00:22:40,129 --> 00:22:45,009
[SPEAKER_03]: You could throw the ball out into the bleachers and right field in St. Louis.

416
00:22:45,890 --> 00:22:51,131
[SPEAKER_03]: I think Chicago I liked as well as any because it was regular.

417
00:22:51,671 --> 00:22:55,151
[SPEAKER_03]: It was about 3.30 I think down each line.

418
00:22:59,676 --> 00:23:02,238
[SPEAKER_01]: You've got to catch us in action here.

419
00:23:03,579 --> 00:23:06,281
[SPEAKER_03]: It was about 3.30 down East Line.

420
00:23:07,121 --> 00:23:09,203
[SPEAKER_03]: If they hit a ball, they had to hit it pretty good.

421
00:23:09,723 --> 00:23:22,411
[SPEAKER_03]: But in St. Louis, even in the Yankees, except out in the afternoon when they started to go out towards it, in those days anyway, it was a short right field, 2.90, something like that.

422
00:23:23,092 --> 00:23:26,094
[SPEAKER_03]: But I think I liked Chicago as well as anything.

423
00:23:27,294 --> 00:23:30,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you think that Ruther hit too many home runs in other ballparks?

424
00:23:32,497 --> 00:23:33,097
[SPEAKER_03]: I think so.

425
00:23:33,518 --> 00:23:35,359
[SPEAKER_03]: Because he didn't hit cheap home runs.

426
00:23:36,239 --> 00:23:38,601
[SPEAKER_03]: I know a lot of these other fellas probably wouldn't.

427
00:23:39,641 --> 00:23:42,063
[SPEAKER_03]: But Ruth, when he hit it more, they were hit.

428
00:23:43,644 --> 00:23:47,286
[SPEAKER_03]: So there was fellas hitting home runs.

429
00:23:47,306 --> 00:23:49,227
[SPEAKER_03]: There's fellas hitting home runs now that

430
00:23:50,143 --> 00:23:52,105
[SPEAKER_03]: They just don't have any business for it.

431
00:23:52,265 --> 00:23:55,207
[SPEAKER_03]: And I think that's because of the ball.

432
00:23:55,507 --> 00:23:58,730
[SPEAKER_01]: And all they emphasize is hitting the wrong ball.

433
00:23:58,870 --> 00:24:01,211
[SPEAKER_01]: They're the fellas that make the money.

434
00:24:01,472 --> 00:24:06,155
[SPEAKER_01]: The scientific game changers then abandon the play for a run or two.

435
00:24:06,576 --> 00:24:09,958
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's what I loved about players like Kong.

436
00:24:10,519 --> 00:24:15,342
[SPEAKER_03]: See, he was a master tactician.

437
00:24:15,382 --> 00:24:17,364
[SPEAKER_03]: He'd walk when he needed the first base.

438
00:24:17,384 --> 00:24:18,565
[SPEAKER_03]: He'd steal second.

439
00:24:19,505 --> 00:24:25,327
[SPEAKER_03]: He'd probably go to third on an infield out and score a sacrifice for us.

440
00:24:25,988 --> 00:24:30,990
[SPEAKER_03]: And in those days, a couple of runs was, they didn't score them like they did now.

441
00:24:31,970 --> 00:24:34,972
[SPEAKER_01]: He could hit them on runs, I guess, but he just didn't try it.

442
00:24:35,612 --> 00:24:37,853
[SPEAKER_03]: He hit one on me one day, I always remember.

443
00:24:38,573 --> 00:24:40,914
[SPEAKER_03]: Something funny, I think, stands out in my mind.

444
00:24:41,474 --> 00:24:42,575
[SPEAKER_03]: It was in St. Louis.

445
00:24:43,635 --> 00:24:45,756
[SPEAKER_03]: And I got him out about three times.

446
00:24:46,637 --> 00:24:47,617
[SPEAKER_03]: And the fourth time,

447
00:24:48,716 --> 00:24:53,839
[SPEAKER_03]: He'd come up and I threw, I used to throw a forkball, you know the one you hold between your fingers?

448
00:24:54,519 --> 00:24:56,701
[SPEAKER_03]: And I threw him a forkball and boy, he hit it.

449
00:24:58,021 --> 00:25:04,085
[SPEAKER_03]: He hit it and he stood on home plate and he said, I knew you'd throw me that sooner or later.

450
00:25:04,105 --> 00:25:06,646
[SPEAKER_03]: So he was right, he hit the thing.

451
00:25:08,647 --> 00:25:09,988
[SPEAKER_03]: But he was one of the greats.

452
00:25:10,128 --> 00:25:13,170
[SPEAKER_03]: He and Ruth, I think, were the greatest fork players I ever saw.

453
00:25:14,611 --> 00:25:15,131
[SPEAKER_03]: They could do.

454
00:25:16,026 --> 00:25:22,290
[SPEAKER_03]: He could do more things than Ruth, but Ruth was a class-bound sub when it comes to subbing.

455
00:25:23,270 --> 00:25:24,971
[SPEAKER_03]: And he was a good outfielder.

456
00:25:25,451 --> 00:25:26,352
[SPEAKER_03]: A good outfielder.

457
00:25:28,413 --> 00:25:30,814
[SPEAKER_03]: They overlooked that because of his hitting.

458
00:25:30,854 --> 00:25:33,116
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, he had a great pitching record for it all.

459
00:25:33,296 --> 00:25:34,456
[SPEAKER_03]: And he was a great pitcher.

460
00:25:34,857 --> 00:25:36,177
[SPEAKER_03]: He was just a natural.

461
00:25:36,698 --> 00:25:38,399
[SPEAKER_03]: Gee, it's a shame he went so soon.

462
00:25:39,459 --> 00:25:42,401
[SPEAKER_03]: He and Gary both, and Gary was younger than him.

463
00:25:44,930 --> 00:25:48,172
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Chris Speaker?

464
00:25:48,412 --> 00:25:50,273
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, another great one.

465
00:25:51,494 --> 00:25:52,495
[SPEAKER_03]: He used to play.

466
00:25:52,575 --> 00:25:56,637
[SPEAKER_03]: In those days, they didn't play way back by the fences when they were hitting.

467
00:25:57,658 --> 00:26:01,040
[SPEAKER_03]: He played a short center field.

468
00:26:01,080 --> 00:26:02,621
[SPEAKER_03]: He could go near the ball with anybody.

469
00:26:02,641 --> 00:26:03,882
[SPEAKER_01]: He could really go back at it.

470
00:26:03,902 --> 00:26:04,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes.

471
00:26:04,623 --> 00:26:11,307
[SPEAKER_01]: I've heard it said he made a couple of unassisted double plays by catching the low-line driver who was second in the run and coming in and doubling him down.

472
00:26:11,327 --> 00:26:14,749
[SPEAKER_03]: I wouldn't doubt it, because he played a short center field.

473
00:26:15,045 --> 00:26:16,606
[SPEAKER_03]: But as you say, he could go back.

474
00:26:16,706 --> 00:26:20,329
[SPEAKER_03]: And they didn't hit the ball like they do now.

475
00:26:20,990 --> 00:26:23,812
[SPEAKER_03]: These outfielders now, they back up against the fence so much.

476
00:26:24,132 --> 00:26:28,056
[SPEAKER_03]: And they have to, because I don't care what they say about the ball.

477
00:26:28,136 --> 00:26:29,317
[SPEAKER_03]: It's been made lively.

478
00:26:29,337 --> 00:26:30,618
[SPEAKER_03]: There's no question about it.

479
00:26:30,758 --> 00:26:31,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Where was it?

480
00:26:31,398 --> 00:26:36,543
[SPEAKER_01]: We were over at Zinbeck's house in Sanford earlier this week.

481
00:26:36,563 --> 00:26:36,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

482
00:26:36,923 --> 00:26:39,345
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know if you remember Zinbeck.

483
00:26:39,465 --> 00:26:39,965
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember.

484
00:26:43,715 --> 00:26:46,978
[SPEAKER_01]: He has a couple of balls cut right through the middle, showing the center.

485
00:26:47,138 --> 00:26:51,482
[SPEAKER_01]: One in 1932, which was getting lively then, and one in the mid-70s.

486
00:26:52,022 --> 00:26:59,749
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can see the different makeup of the ball, which made this 76 or 70 ball so far more lively, you know.

487
00:26:59,789 --> 00:27:00,770
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, the center of it.

488
00:27:01,510 --> 00:27:03,472
[SPEAKER_03]: They claim it was the center.

489
00:27:03,812 --> 00:27:07,796
[SPEAKER_03]: They used to have a corks in it, which was lively enough, and then they had some kind of

490
00:27:08,447 --> 00:27:11,868
[SPEAKER_03]: a rubber with some kind of a liquid inside.

491
00:27:12,969 --> 00:27:15,050
[SPEAKER_03]: What kind did he have?

492
00:27:15,630 --> 00:27:26,035
[SPEAKER_00]: He had the one with the big cork, and then the other one was almost solid rubber, with just a little teeny-weeny bit of cork in the center.

493
00:27:26,075 --> 00:27:32,777
[SPEAKER_03]: They've purposely made them lively because, as you said, people want to see that home run, that's what they pay for.

494
00:27:33,037 --> 00:27:34,318
[SPEAKER_01]: It is a thrill.

495
00:27:34,398 --> 00:27:35,238
[SPEAKER_03]: Look at Kingman.

496
00:27:37,235 --> 00:27:38,336
[SPEAKER_01]: He strikes out.

497
00:27:38,576 --> 00:27:46,083
[SPEAKER_03]: He looks terrible at that bat, and at the same time, if you give him anywhere where he can hit it, he'll hit it out of the park.

498
00:27:46,103 --> 00:27:47,624
[SPEAKER_03]: And there's a lot of hitters like that.

499
00:27:48,605 --> 00:27:54,329
[SPEAKER_03]: As long as they hit that long board, and they go for that because that's where the money is.

500
00:27:54,409 --> 00:27:56,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Ralph Conner said they drive Cadillacs.

501
00:27:56,551 --> 00:27:57,032
[SPEAKER_01]: That's right.

502
00:27:57,392 --> 00:27:59,433
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Lig Park in Cleveland?

503
00:27:59,534 --> 00:28:02,456
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of a tough right field there, but they're short ones.

504
00:28:03,012 --> 00:28:03,512
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where I grew up.

505
00:28:03,772 --> 00:28:06,633
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh yes, I remember it was a short right field first.

506
00:28:06,653 --> 00:28:08,114
[SPEAKER_03]: They had a big screen up there.

507
00:28:08,214 --> 00:28:11,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, 290 feet and about a 40 foot screen.

508
00:28:11,695 --> 00:28:14,196
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you had to be careful there.

509
00:28:14,276 --> 00:28:15,336
[SPEAKER_03]: It was kind of tough there.

510
00:28:15,777 --> 00:28:20,218
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, of course, you had to change your style of pitching if you were pitching in a ballpark like that.

511
00:28:20,718 --> 00:28:24,420
[SPEAKER_03]: And in Boston, they had a short left field.

512
00:28:24,440 --> 00:28:25,420
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you change it?

513
00:28:25,460 --> 00:28:28,301
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, suppose a guy is an inside hitter.

514
00:28:29,120 --> 00:28:34,923
[SPEAKER_03]: You couldn't dare, you wouldn't dare throw an inside ball in Boston.

515
00:28:35,583 --> 00:28:38,885
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, he isn't an insider, he's an outsider.

516
00:28:39,505 --> 00:28:46,749
[SPEAKER_03]: But still, you've got to throw to his strength, because if you threw it inside, I could pop one over there myself.

517
00:28:46,829 --> 00:28:56,994
[SPEAKER_03]: I remember Walter Johnson hit one up there when I was in Boston, beat some club, hit it up on that screen and right field, and he couldn't hit.

518
00:29:01,186 --> 00:29:03,026
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, did you like your years in St. Louis?

519
00:29:04,207 --> 00:29:06,927
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, I liked St. Louis.

520
00:29:07,968 --> 00:29:09,068
[SPEAKER_03]: That's where I met my wife.

521
00:29:09,648 --> 00:29:11,269
[SPEAKER_03]: I've got to say I liked it.

522
00:29:11,329 --> 00:29:15,029
[SPEAKER_03]: How did you meet her?

523
00:29:16,270 --> 00:29:17,410
[SPEAKER_03]: You know Danny McFady?

524
00:29:17,710 --> 00:29:17,930
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

525
00:29:18,790 --> 00:29:19,931
[SPEAKER_03]: He's my brother-in-law.

526
00:29:19,951 --> 00:29:20,351
[SPEAKER_03]: Is that right?

527
00:29:20,371 --> 00:29:20,631
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

528
00:29:21,411 --> 00:29:25,292
[SPEAKER_03]: He and my wife, my wife and his wife are sisters.

529
00:29:25,885 --> 00:29:27,286
[SPEAKER_03]: In fact, she lives here in Franklin.

530
00:29:27,306 --> 00:29:28,287
[SPEAKER_03]: She moved down here.

531
00:29:28,307 --> 00:29:32,831
[SPEAKER_03]: Because Danny died in 1966, I think.

532
00:29:32,871 --> 00:29:35,753
[SPEAKER_03]: They lived in Maine, so she moved down here.

533
00:29:36,153 --> 00:29:40,277
[SPEAKER_03]: So... Did he introduce you to... No.

534
00:29:41,938 --> 00:29:48,484
[SPEAKER_03]: There was some other player on the St. Louis club that knew her sister and introduced me to her.

535
00:29:50,045 --> 00:29:52,307
[SPEAKER_03]: So we've been married for 52 years.

536
00:29:56,232 --> 00:29:59,833
[SPEAKER_01]: She liked baseball from the starter.

537
00:29:59,853 --> 00:30:03,874
[SPEAKER_03]: She didn't know much about it, but she learned to like it.

538
00:30:05,815 --> 00:30:07,415
[SPEAKER_03]: She's interested in it now.

539
00:30:07,616 --> 00:30:14,758
[SPEAKER_03]: In fact, I'm probably as big a fan now as I ever was, because that thing there will make a fan out of you.

540
00:30:14,798 --> 00:30:17,679
[SPEAKER_03]: Especially when you retire and don't have much to do.

541
00:30:18,619 --> 00:30:19,979
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm very much interested.

542
00:30:20,199 --> 00:30:22,120
[SPEAKER_03]: I grew up for most any kind of sport.

543
00:30:23,573 --> 00:30:26,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything with wrestling.

544
00:30:27,436 --> 00:30:31,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Boxing was something that was very popular in the 20s, wasn't it?

545
00:30:31,459 --> 00:30:34,281
[SPEAKER_03]: It was popular, and I took a liking to boxing.

546
00:30:34,301 --> 00:30:42,207
[SPEAKER_03]: I like boxing now, good boxing, but some of these things they put on there, they're not much.

547
00:30:42,427 --> 00:30:46,810
[SPEAKER_03]: I like football, but of course you get saturated with it down here.

548
00:30:46,950 --> 00:30:49,873
[SPEAKER_03]: They give you too much, but I don't think it does them any good.

549
00:30:51,514 --> 00:30:52,675
[SPEAKER_03]: This club over here in

550
00:30:55,188 --> 00:30:55,848
[SPEAKER_03]: How about that?

551
00:30:56,368 --> 00:30:57,489
[SPEAKER_03]: It's come from nowhere.

552
00:30:57,529 --> 00:30:59,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Four years ago, they were losing everything.

553
00:30:59,470 --> 00:31:00,811
[SPEAKER_03]: That's remarkable.

554
00:31:01,692 --> 00:31:07,495
[SPEAKER_03]: But this fellow McKay, when they brought him to California, he said, give him five years.

555
00:31:07,575 --> 00:31:09,756
[SPEAKER_03]: And he lost 26 straight games.

556
00:31:09,836 --> 00:31:12,498
[SPEAKER_03]: Boy, they got never in a hundred years.

557
00:31:12,518 --> 00:31:13,758
[SPEAKER_03]: What do you get for that?

558
00:31:13,918 --> 00:31:14,979
[SPEAKER_03]: That's a good drafting.

559
00:31:15,539 --> 00:31:16,280
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, he drafts.

560
00:31:16,300 --> 00:31:19,522
[SPEAKER_03]: Somebody knows he's a great guy for defense.

561
00:31:20,342 --> 00:31:21,723
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's what made that club.

562
00:31:22,932 --> 00:31:24,753
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you play any football in high school?

563
00:31:24,994 --> 00:31:27,635
[SPEAKER_03]: I played it till I got a bad knee and then I quit.

564
00:31:28,436 --> 00:31:30,317
[SPEAKER_03]: I still can feel it sometimes.

565
00:31:30,858 --> 00:31:32,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Was it bothering you in baseball?

566
00:31:32,799 --> 00:31:33,560
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it never did.

567
00:31:34,140 --> 00:31:35,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you ever have sore arm problems?

568
00:31:35,541 --> 00:31:42,206
[SPEAKER_03]: Never had a sore arm, doggone it, until the last little 1930 in Boston.

569
00:31:43,067 --> 00:31:45,829
[SPEAKER_03]: I came up with a sore arm and I never did get over it.

570
00:31:46,389 --> 00:31:50,732
[SPEAKER_03]: In fact, I bluffed my way through for three more years over in Chicago.

571
00:31:50,872 --> 00:31:51,873
[SPEAKER_03]: I didn't have anything.

572
00:31:58,205 --> 00:31:59,926
[SPEAKER_01]: What about the trade to Washington?

573
00:32:00,066 --> 00:32:05,150
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just as easily forget that.

574
00:32:05,410 --> 00:32:08,833
[SPEAKER_03]: Because I went to Washington and I had a terrible year.

575
00:32:08,853 --> 00:32:09,514
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, why?

576
00:32:10,174 --> 00:32:17,600
[SPEAKER_03]: The only reason I can think of is 27 was I had a good year, 27 with St. Louis.

577
00:32:18,541 --> 00:32:21,743
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, with that club it would be considered a good year.

578
00:32:22,838 --> 00:32:28,080
[SPEAKER_03]: And I went to Washington, of course, they expected I was going to do the same thing.

579
00:32:28,100 --> 00:32:28,781
[SPEAKER_03]: Were you traded?

580
00:32:29,141 --> 00:32:30,041
[SPEAKER_03]: I was traded, yes.

581
00:32:30,061 --> 00:32:34,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you remember?

582
00:32:34,223 --> 00:32:35,244
[SPEAKER_03]: Gee, I don't remember.

583
00:32:36,544 --> 00:32:41,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Were you excited about it, or unhappy, or didn't you just take it in stride?

584
00:32:41,707 --> 00:32:43,247
[SPEAKER_03]: No, I took it in stride.

585
00:32:43,347 --> 00:32:49,330
[SPEAKER_03]: Luckily, I was traded four or five times, but every time I was traded, it was after the season was over.

586
00:32:49,370 --> 00:32:51,011
[SPEAKER_03]: I was never traded during the season.

587
00:32:51,662 --> 00:32:58,727
[SPEAKER_03]: which is tough up here, because you break up living in Anderson or in whatever city you're in.

588
00:32:59,188 --> 00:33:01,650
[SPEAKER_03]: But I was always traded after the season was over.

589
00:33:02,470 --> 00:33:07,914
[SPEAKER_03]: So I went over to Washington, and the year before I had a pretty good year in St. Louis.

590
00:33:08,875 --> 00:33:12,898
[SPEAKER_03]: And I whooped it up that winter, thought I was pretty good.

591
00:33:13,679 --> 00:33:14,559
[SPEAKER_03]: And I got fat.

592
00:33:16,681 --> 00:33:18,742
[SPEAKER_02]: And when I went over there, boy, they ran me.

593
00:33:19,503 --> 00:33:20,964
[SPEAKER_03]: Bump Hadley, remember him?

594
00:33:21,311 --> 00:33:23,912
[SPEAKER_03]: Bump was over there too, and he was fat.

595
00:33:24,092 --> 00:33:28,435
[SPEAKER_03]: And they rained us in spring training until our tongues were numb.

596
00:33:28,475 --> 00:33:30,275
[SPEAKER_03]: We couldn't.

597
00:33:30,375 --> 00:33:33,697
[SPEAKER_03]: And I use that for an excuse, I don't know if it is or not.

598
00:33:34,518 --> 00:33:37,099
[SPEAKER_03]: But I didn't have a good year at all over there.

599
00:33:37,299 --> 00:33:38,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Was Walter Johnson managing then?

600
00:33:39,040 --> 00:33:40,360
[SPEAKER_03]: No, Bucky Harris.

601
00:33:40,420 --> 00:33:40,861
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, Harris.

602
00:33:41,781 --> 00:33:45,703
[SPEAKER_03]: And Harris didn't think much of me, so I didn't blame him.

603
00:33:47,116 --> 00:33:49,238
[SPEAKER_03]: So I was traded then to Boston.

604
00:33:49,338 --> 00:33:50,819
[SPEAKER_03]: I was happiest in Boston.

605
00:33:50,919 --> 00:33:53,161
[SPEAKER_03]: I had some good balls.

