[SPEAKER_02]: He always got it back, I think.

812
00:44:23,284 --> 00:44:24,125
[SPEAKER_02]: He was a great hitter.

813
00:44:24,145 --> 00:44:27,507
[SPEAKER_02]: He was a good ball player besides.

814
00:44:27,767 --> 00:44:30,868
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, they overlooked some of these fellas like Ruth, for instance.

815
00:44:31,729 --> 00:44:37,372
[SPEAKER_02]: He was a fine outfielder, but he hit so good that they just talked about nothing but a setting.

816
00:44:39,143 --> 00:44:41,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Who came after Kerrigan?

817
00:44:41,544 --> 00:44:41,804
[SPEAKER_02]: Bill?

818
00:44:41,844 --> 00:44:44,385
[SPEAKER_02]: He was the coach.

819
00:44:44,405 --> 00:44:46,385
[SPEAKER_02]: Real nice guy, but he wasn't so manly.

820
00:44:46,505 --> 00:44:50,307
[SPEAKER_02]: Of course, managing to me is, if you got the fellas to like you,

821
00:45:01,174 --> 00:45:05,156
[SPEAKER_02]: And he showed a little authority with this bombastic stuff and mocking somebody.

822
00:45:05,236 --> 00:45:05,797
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't believe him.

823
00:45:05,817 --> 00:45:06,657
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't either.

824
00:45:07,018 --> 00:45:08,118
[SPEAKER_02]: That's good for the cameras.

825
00:45:09,079 --> 00:45:12,901
[SPEAKER_02]: But I don't believe him.

826
00:45:13,121 --> 00:45:16,323
[SPEAKER_01]: It required knowing your players and getting their respect.

827
00:45:16,363 --> 00:45:17,124
[SPEAKER_02]: No, and that's it.

828
00:45:17,144 --> 00:45:20,345
[SPEAKER_02]: The respect is everything because they'll go out and do things for you.

829
00:45:20,706 --> 00:45:23,347
[SPEAKER_02]: And if they don't, they're crazy, because they're probably doing it for themselves.

830
00:45:23,848 --> 00:45:28,410
[SPEAKER_02]: If you get them into everything, you think she knows what you're doing, you won't have any trouble.

831
00:45:30,323 --> 00:45:33,245
[SPEAKER_02]: If you've got the horses, if you don't, you might as well just quit.

832
00:45:34,085 --> 00:45:42,830
[SPEAKER_03]: Because they can't... Bill Kerrigan, in fact, well, he managed the Red Sox way back in 1919, and then got out and then brought them back.

833
00:45:57,719 --> 00:45:58,340
[SPEAKER_03]: He said to me,

834
00:45:59,569 --> 00:46:07,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, we're talking about the Boston team in 1930 and 31 there, 29.

835
00:46:08,151 --> 00:46:15,453
[SPEAKER_01]: And some of the players mentioned Russ Garrett, who had a couple of good years, I guess, maybe.

836
00:46:15,513 --> 00:46:18,634
[SPEAKER_03]: He had good years, and he was a real good ball player in AA.

837
00:46:20,534 --> 00:46:22,095
[SPEAKER_03]: I still get in touch with Russ.

838
00:46:22,675 --> 00:46:23,355
[SPEAKER_03]: He called me.

839
00:46:24,088 --> 00:46:25,148
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, last year he called me.

840
00:46:25,168 --> 00:46:26,528
[SPEAKER_03]: I expected he might come down.

841
00:46:27,009 --> 00:46:28,249
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, he's from Pensacola.

842
00:46:28,709 --> 00:46:30,829
[SPEAKER_03]: He's lived there all his life, I guess.

843
00:46:31,949 --> 00:46:33,310
[SPEAKER_03]: And he called me last year.

844
00:46:33,330 --> 00:46:36,010
[SPEAKER_03]: I had a card from him, Christmas card, this year.

845
00:46:36,030 --> 00:46:37,250
[SPEAKER_03]: We get a card every year.

846
00:46:38,091 --> 00:46:41,111
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm hoping that he might make a trip down this way one day.

847
00:46:42,191 --> 00:46:43,772
[SPEAKER_03]: Because he was a real good guy.

848
00:46:44,632 --> 00:46:47,412
[SPEAKER_01]: And Earl Webb had his great year we mentioned there.

849
00:46:47,452 --> 00:46:50,353
[SPEAKER_03]: Webb had to set the record for doubles that year.

850
00:46:50,393 --> 00:46:51,933
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't remember which year it was.

851
00:46:53,929 --> 00:46:56,249
[SPEAKER_01]: Tell the story again, if you would, about the grueling watches.

852
00:46:57,050 --> 00:47:00,371
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, we got Webby Kepes and grueling watches.

853
00:47:00,391 --> 00:47:02,151
[SPEAKER_03]: They gave one for every home run.

854
00:47:03,132 --> 00:47:06,933
[SPEAKER_03]: So practically everybody in the club had one.

855
00:47:06,973 --> 00:47:10,835
[SPEAKER_03]: Not that they hit the home run, but Webby hit them for them.

856
00:47:13,055 --> 00:47:15,396
[SPEAKER_01]: How about your infield in those days?

857
00:47:16,457 --> 00:47:19,498
[SPEAKER_03]: Let's see, we had Phil Tote on first base.

858
00:47:20,724 --> 00:47:22,545
[SPEAKER_03]: Bill Reagan and Second Base.

859
00:47:24,386 --> 00:47:28,189
[SPEAKER_03]: I think Reeves came over there from Washington.

860
00:47:28,229 --> 00:47:30,090
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Hal Ryan, was he with that team?

861
00:47:30,110 --> 00:47:31,031
[SPEAKER_03]: Hal Ryan, that's right.

862
00:47:31,071 --> 00:47:32,271
[SPEAKER_03]: He was the shortstop.

863
00:47:33,432 --> 00:47:37,495
[SPEAKER_03]: He'd been with Pittsburgh, I guess.

864
00:47:37,515 --> 00:47:37,775
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

865
00:47:38,735 --> 00:47:41,957
[SPEAKER_03]: And I think... How about Pickering?

866
00:47:41,977 --> 00:47:43,759
[SPEAKER_03]: Was there a Pickering then?

867
00:47:43,859 --> 00:47:45,940
[SPEAKER_03]: Pickering was a catcher, I think.

868
00:47:47,387 --> 00:47:50,628
[SPEAKER_03]: But he wasn't with them, not with Boston.

869
00:47:52,209 --> 00:47:53,969
[SPEAKER_03]: I was trying to think who was on third base.

870
00:47:54,089 --> 00:47:56,850
[SPEAKER_03]: Odie Miller played third.

871
00:47:57,990 --> 00:48:00,691
[SPEAKER_03]: Was he with Boston?

872
00:48:01,752 --> 00:48:02,192
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know.

873
00:48:02,272 --> 00:48:03,892
[SPEAKER_03]: I can't say.

874
00:48:05,133 --> 00:48:06,533
[SPEAKER_03]: But I don't remember who was there.

875
00:48:08,494 --> 00:48:11,995
[SPEAKER_01]: How did the town support the team in those days?

876
00:48:12,095 --> 00:48:13,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, Boston's a great ballpark.

877
00:48:14,095 --> 00:48:15,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Even when the team wasn't doing so well.

878
00:48:16,921 --> 00:48:22,542
[SPEAKER_03]: We would sell it to cellar dwellers all the time, but they'd still come out there.

879
00:48:22,562 --> 00:48:24,442
[SPEAKER_01]: How did that affect the team's morale?

880
00:48:25,102 --> 00:48:26,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it doesn't help it any.

881
00:48:27,563 --> 00:48:35,284
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, I used to play in St. Louis on one Saturday, I believe it was on a Saturday, and there was 400 and some people at the ball game.

882
00:48:37,304 --> 00:48:40,625
[SPEAKER_03]: And Washington came into town, and Nick Altra.

883
00:48:41,585 --> 00:48:44,146
[SPEAKER_03]: They didn't have a public announcer in those days.

884
00:48:45,126 --> 00:48:46,386
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, the system.

885
00:48:47,138 --> 00:48:49,319
[SPEAKER_03]: We had the old megaphone.

886
00:48:49,379 --> 00:48:57,941
[SPEAKER_03]: Nick got the megaphone and he asked all the folks to please move into one station so they could hear him.

887
00:48:58,481 --> 00:48:59,681
[SPEAKER_03]: He was a funny guy.

888
00:49:00,721 --> 00:49:06,323
[SPEAKER_01]: He and Al Trotter went to Washington when I was over there.

889
00:49:06,343 --> 00:49:08,443
[SPEAKER_03]: They used to draw people and sell them.

890
00:49:08,843 --> 00:49:15,565
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember seeing them playing doubleheaders in Cleveland when they were coaching, put on the stunts between the games.

891
00:49:17,806 --> 00:49:21,449
[SPEAKER_01]: They were good comedians, both of them.

892
00:49:21,930 --> 00:49:23,851
[SPEAKER_01]: They knew their baseball too, I guess.

893
00:49:24,031 --> 00:49:26,994
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh yeah, Nick was a top-notch pitcher.

894
00:49:28,215 --> 00:49:31,478
[SPEAKER_03]: I never knew too much about Shaq.

895
00:49:31,518 --> 00:49:33,239
[SPEAKER_03]: I think he was a pitcher, I don't know.

896
00:49:33,420 --> 00:49:40,246
[SPEAKER_01]: When they were coaching you, or coaching in the lines there, would they mix in a lot of their humor with the serious stuff?

897
00:49:40,346 --> 00:49:42,468
[SPEAKER_03]: Not too much while they were on the lines.

898
00:49:44,497 --> 00:49:45,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Once in a while.

899
00:49:45,277 --> 00:49:49,579
[SPEAKER_01]: You'd think they're so well known as comedians it'd be hard to ever be able to take them seriously.

900
00:49:49,840 --> 00:49:54,722
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, they didn't always coach on mornings either.

901
00:49:55,922 --> 00:50:10,849
[SPEAKER_03]: The funniest thing I saw on the coaching was one day over in Brooklyn, I think that was before I went up there, Casey Stengel was coaching over in Brooklyn.

902
00:50:14,438 --> 00:50:16,879
[SPEAKER_03]: It rained like the day, and they held up the game.

903
00:50:18,180 --> 00:50:20,861
[SPEAKER_03]: Of course, they brought the tarpaulin out and put it on.

904
00:50:20,881 --> 00:50:24,402
[SPEAKER_03]: He went out there with a bucket and a big sponge.

905
00:50:26,443 --> 00:50:28,644
[SPEAKER_03]: Squeezing it in the bucket and throwing it away.

906
00:50:28,944 --> 00:50:30,245
[SPEAKER_03]: And that was really fun.

907
00:50:30,905 --> 00:50:36,307
[SPEAKER_03]: And only he could do it.

908
00:50:36,728 --> 00:50:39,389
[SPEAKER_03]: Another great clown was Rabbit Maranville.

909
00:50:40,409 --> 00:50:41,990
[SPEAKER_03]: One day he'd get in some kind of a...

910
00:50:42,687 --> 00:50:44,589
[SPEAKER_03]: deal there where they had to hold up the game.

911
00:50:45,930 --> 00:50:56,379
[SPEAKER_03]: Or they'd get in an argument, there'd be two fellas getting in an argument or arguing with the umpire and he'd go around sparring with himself and finally hit himself.

912
00:50:56,719 --> 00:50:58,521
[SPEAKER_03]: You had to see it to appreciate it.

913
00:51:01,604 --> 00:51:02,865
[SPEAKER_01]: How'd you get along with umpires?

914
00:51:03,645 --> 00:51:03,946
[SPEAKER_03]: Good.

915
00:51:04,826 --> 00:51:08,610
[SPEAKER_03]: I think I was only throwing out one game that I had.

916
00:51:10,172 --> 00:51:13,054
[SPEAKER_03]: But I got along good.

917
00:51:14,354 --> 00:51:16,275
[SPEAKER_03]: I was smart enough to deal with them.

918
00:51:16,335 --> 00:51:20,297
[SPEAKER_03]: I didn't want to get on the wrong side of them because they could hurt you.

919
00:51:21,138 --> 00:51:24,240
[SPEAKER_03]: They know if they give you a little bit here, a little bit there.

920
00:51:25,880 --> 00:51:28,802
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you have any favorites?

921
00:51:28,822 --> 00:51:35,906
[SPEAKER_03]: The best one I remember was, I can't think of his name.

922
00:51:38,180 --> 00:51:41,362
[SPEAKER_03]: I had some good umpires, but I was just pointing this one out.

923
00:51:41,422 --> 00:51:44,884
[SPEAKER_03]: Billy Evans.

924
00:51:47,346 --> 00:51:48,246
[SPEAKER_03]: He was one of them.

925
00:51:48,306 --> 00:51:49,087
[SPEAKER_03]: He knew the book.

926
00:51:49,307 --> 00:51:51,348
[SPEAKER_03]: He was smart.

927
00:51:51,888 --> 00:51:52,969
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a writer too.

928
00:51:53,069 --> 00:51:54,370
[SPEAKER_03]: He wrote columns all the time.

929
00:51:54,390 --> 00:51:55,331
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what I say.

930
00:51:55,351 --> 00:51:56,231
[SPEAKER_03]: He was smart.

931
00:51:56,271 --> 00:51:57,452
[SPEAKER_03]: He knew what it was all about.

932
00:51:57,612 --> 00:52:04,296
[SPEAKER_03]: If there was any question about the legality of a play or something, he usually had it at his fingertips.

933
00:52:06,163 --> 00:52:08,103
[SPEAKER_01]: They do a pretty good job, you think?

934
00:52:08,423 --> 00:52:10,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, they do a marvelous job.

935
00:52:10,924 --> 00:52:12,904
[SPEAKER_03]: They're wrong with everybody.

936
00:52:13,004 --> 00:52:17,365
[SPEAKER_03]: No matter what happens, he's got to be wrong with one or the other.

937
00:52:18,245 --> 00:52:22,706
[SPEAKER_03]: And this business nowadays, they exaggerate it, of course.

938
00:52:23,006 --> 00:52:27,027
[SPEAKER_03]: As I say, that camera, that thing there, makes a different ballgame.

939
00:52:27,047 --> 00:52:29,147
[SPEAKER_03]: But look at Earl Weaver.

940
00:52:29,907 --> 00:52:33,008
[SPEAKER_03]: He go out there, and oh boy, he's a great actor.

941
00:52:33,771 --> 00:52:38,392
[SPEAKER_03]: And from what I understand, I don't know, but they claim he's not a bad fellow at all.

942
00:52:39,072 --> 00:52:43,274
[SPEAKER_03]: But you'd think he was the meanest guy in the world when he gets on with those little guys.

943
00:52:47,275 --> 00:52:49,435
[SPEAKER_03]: And Billy Martin loves that camera, too.

944
00:52:52,776 --> 00:52:54,097
[SPEAKER_03]: Billy's out right now, I mean.

945
00:52:54,137 --> 00:52:56,157
[SPEAKER_03]: I hope he stays out, so... Yeah.

946
00:52:56,617 --> 00:52:57,437
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't...

947
00:52:57,478 --> 00:52:58,438
[SPEAKER_03]: There's no point to...

948
00:52:58,518 --> 00:52:58,938
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean...

949
00:53:00,832 --> 00:53:03,413
[SPEAKER_03]: He's had all the chances in the world, gee.

950
00:53:03,433 --> 00:53:07,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you ever play a night ball?

951
00:53:09,495 --> 00:53:29,423
[SPEAKER_03]: No, that came in in 1935, I think it came in, because I remember I went over and worked out with the Cincinnati Club, trying to hook on with them.

952
00:53:30,483 --> 00:53:33,104
[SPEAKER_03]: And McPhail was running the club then.

953
00:53:33,144 --> 00:53:34,384
[SPEAKER_03]: He was the boss.

954
00:53:35,585 --> 00:53:39,086
[SPEAKER_03]: And he asked me what I thought of night baseball.

955
00:53:39,126 --> 00:53:40,967
[SPEAKER_03]: I said, they're going to play night baseball.

956
00:53:40,987 --> 00:53:41,767
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, what do you think?

957
00:53:43,047 --> 00:53:45,828
[SPEAKER_03]: I told him I thought it would be a pretty good idea.

958
00:53:46,709 --> 00:53:49,090
[SPEAKER_03]: There would be a lot of people who couldn't get to them in the afternoon.

959
00:53:49,190 --> 00:53:51,230
[SPEAKER_03]: And of course, it turned out to be a great thing.

960
00:53:52,131 --> 00:53:59,053
[SPEAKER_03]: And I had never seen a night ball game except once down in Terre Haute, Indiana when I went down there.

961
00:53:59,447 --> 00:54:01,468
[SPEAKER_03]: When I was with the Red Sox, I came up with a bad arm.

962
00:54:01,488 --> 00:54:05,730
[SPEAKER_03]: They sent me down there to this arm doctor down there, Doc Ferguson.

963
00:54:05,770 --> 00:54:07,631
[SPEAKER_03]: He was an arm specialist.

964
00:54:08,272 --> 00:54:10,393
[SPEAKER_03]: And he couldn't do anything for me.

965
00:54:10,433 --> 00:54:11,854
[SPEAKER_03]: We heard about Bonesetter Reese.

966
00:54:12,314 --> 00:54:13,214
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, sure.

967
00:54:13,395 --> 00:54:14,475
[SPEAKER_03]: He was very famous.

968
00:54:15,356 --> 00:54:20,758
[SPEAKER_03]: But this fellow and his brother, I think, finally, one of the two of them, they were arm specialists.

969
00:54:20,799 --> 00:54:23,160
[SPEAKER_03]: But he couldn't do anything for me.

970
00:54:23,480 --> 00:54:24,501
[SPEAKER_03]: My arm was shot.

971
00:54:25,201 --> 00:54:25,981
[SPEAKER_01]: What year was that?

972
00:54:26,001 --> 00:54:26,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you remember?

973
00:54:26,982 --> 00:54:27,782
[SPEAKER_03]: That was in 1930.

974
00:54:29,690 --> 00:54:33,072
[SPEAKER_03]: Thirty, no, yeah, thirty.

975
00:54:33,132 --> 00:54:34,612
[SPEAKER_03]: No, thirty-one, thirty-one.

976
00:54:35,072 --> 00:54:39,154
[SPEAKER_03]: Because I was traded over to Chicago in thirty-two.

977
00:54:40,655 --> 00:54:44,677
[SPEAKER_03]: And thirty-two, three, and four, I didn't have it anymore.

978
00:54:44,697 --> 00:54:48,378
[SPEAKER_03]: I was just bluffing my way through more than anything.

979
00:54:49,619 --> 00:54:51,640
[SPEAKER_03]: I was lucky to hang on that long, I guess.

980
00:54:51,680 --> 00:54:52,760
[SPEAKER_03]: How could you bluff, though?

981
00:54:52,820 --> 00:54:54,241
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you had to get these guys out.

982
00:54:54,481 --> 00:54:57,382
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, you didn't have to have overpowering stuff.

983
00:54:57,402 --> 00:54:58,903
[SPEAKER_03]: You knew how to pitch.

984
00:54:59,908 --> 00:55:05,891
[SPEAKER_03]: Of course, you didn't have too much success at it, but you had enough to get by.

985
00:55:07,352 --> 00:55:10,194
[SPEAKER_03]: What was your best pitch in your better days?

986
00:55:10,294 --> 00:55:11,494
[SPEAKER_03]: I think a fastball.

987
00:55:12,235 --> 00:55:15,517
[SPEAKER_03]: I used to throw a football.

988
00:55:15,737 --> 00:55:19,339
[SPEAKER_03]: One day I'd have a lot of luck with it, and the next day I'd wonder whether who.

989
00:55:19,399 --> 00:55:20,379
[SPEAKER_03]: It wasn't like Suda.

990
00:55:20,980 --> 00:55:22,581
[SPEAKER_03]: Suda can throw that thing every day.

991
00:55:23,741 --> 00:55:26,603
[SPEAKER_03]: But one day I would have luck with it, and another day I wouldn't.

992
00:55:27,990 --> 00:55:29,911
[SPEAKER_03]: When you didn't have it, you just wouldn't use it.

993
00:55:29,971 --> 00:55:31,272
[SPEAKER_03]: You'd go to something else.

994
00:55:32,653 --> 00:55:34,053
[SPEAKER_03]: But I think of fastballs.

995
00:55:35,234 --> 00:55:48,641
[SPEAKER_03]: And I also think if I had the coaching in those days, where they could tell you things, you know, that you wouldn't know yourself or none of the other players in the world, that it would help you.

996
00:55:49,462 --> 00:55:55,425
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, a good, smart pitcher would watch you and tell you what you might be doing.

997
00:55:57,470 --> 00:55:59,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you call most of your own games as well?

998
00:56:01,073 --> 00:56:02,574
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, we got along.

999
00:56:02,754 --> 00:56:06,557
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, Barry, Charlie Barry, was my catcher over in Boston.

1000
00:56:07,578 --> 00:56:10,721
[SPEAKER_03]: And he and I got along good, very good.

1001
00:56:11,141 --> 00:56:14,364
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, he'd shake me off once in a while, but not too much.

1002
00:56:14,944 --> 00:56:18,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Over in St. Louis, I pitched most of my own games.

1003
00:56:18,807 --> 00:56:23,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Did the manager interfere much in any of these teams you played with about the pitching?

1004
00:56:24,552 --> 00:56:24,692
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

1005
00:56:28,558 --> 00:56:31,099
[SPEAKER_03]: They figured if you were up there, you should know what you were doing.

1006
00:56:32,279 --> 00:56:34,620
[SPEAKER_03]: Wally Shang was a catcher over there.

1007
00:56:35,600 --> 00:56:36,201
[SPEAKER_03]: Remember him?

1008
00:56:36,921 --> 00:56:38,541
[SPEAKER_03]: He was a catcher over in New York.

1009
00:56:40,022 --> 00:56:45,184
