1216
01:05:59,698 --> 01:06:01,620
[SPEAKER_03]: Got in about 30 some games that year.

1217
01:06:02,553 --> 01:06:04,955
[SPEAKER_03]: And from then on, I was a regular.

1218
01:06:05,075 --> 01:06:08,117
[SPEAKER_03]: And I was a regular in every club I was with until the time I quit.

1219
01:06:08,617 --> 01:06:14,381
[SPEAKER_03]: Regardless how lousy I was, I was still a regular.

1220
01:06:16,703 --> 01:06:19,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you won a few games over there with Chicago.

1221
01:06:20,926 --> 01:06:21,426
[SPEAKER_03]: Not many.

1222
01:06:22,927 --> 01:06:23,948
[SPEAKER_01]: You lost a few, too.

1223
01:06:24,208 --> 01:06:25,349
[SPEAKER_03]: We lost a few, too.

1224
01:06:25,929 --> 01:06:26,890
[SPEAKER_03]: I think we all did.

1225
01:06:27,330 --> 01:06:30,572
[SPEAKER_03]: They had one of the greatest pitches in the business over there at 10 yards.

1226
01:06:30,713 --> 01:06:31,153
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, yeah.

1227
01:06:45,837 --> 01:06:49,938
[SPEAKER_03]: I think he won 27 games, even if that's Chicago.

1228
01:06:51,379 --> 01:06:53,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you ever come close to a no-hitter?

1229
01:06:55,220 --> 01:06:56,960
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I was talking the other day.

1230
01:06:58,280 --> 01:07:05,022
[SPEAKER_03]: My brother passed on last February, a year ago.

1231
01:07:05,082 --> 01:07:08,183
[SPEAKER_03]: No, last February.

1232
01:07:08,303 --> 01:07:12,784
[SPEAKER_03]: And they tell the story, of course, after a guy goes and they start reminiscing.

1233
01:07:13,605 --> 01:07:14,845
[SPEAKER_03]: And they were talking about him.

1234
01:07:16,453 --> 01:07:19,895
[SPEAKER_03]: getting a hit off me in St. Louis when he was with Boston.

1235
01:07:19,935 --> 01:07:21,175
[SPEAKER_03]: I was pitching to St. Louis.

1236
01:07:21,916 --> 01:07:26,538
[SPEAKER_03]: And I don't remember, they tell the story, that I had a no-hitter going for seven innings.

1237
01:07:27,538 --> 01:07:28,799
[SPEAKER_02]: And he got the first hitter.

1238
01:07:28,819 --> 01:07:30,979
[SPEAKER_03]: Your own brother broke it up.

1239
01:07:31,020 --> 01:07:32,340
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know how true that was.

1240
01:07:32,380 --> 01:07:35,141
[SPEAKER_03]: I know it was a low-hit ballgame and he did get the hit.

1241
01:07:35,201 --> 01:07:35,862
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, I see.

1242
01:07:36,262 --> 01:07:39,923
[SPEAKER_03]: But I don't remember whether it was going towards a no-hitter or not.

1243
01:07:40,023 --> 01:07:43,345
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't really need it.

1244
01:07:44,370 --> 01:07:46,311
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't need a glass.

1245
01:07:46,411 --> 01:07:48,213
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a real tall beer, isn't it?

1246
01:07:49,053 --> 01:07:49,393
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

1247
01:07:50,094 --> 01:07:52,836
[SPEAKER_03]: She and I usually split one.

1248
01:07:52,856 --> 01:07:54,657
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the extent of my drinking.

1249
01:07:55,738 --> 01:07:57,659
[SPEAKER_03]: I drink a beer, we have a beer every day.

1250
01:07:57,959 --> 01:08:01,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Was there much drinking in any of those teams?

1251
01:08:03,423 --> 01:08:05,284
[SPEAKER_03]: Certain ones, certain ones.

1252
01:08:06,605 --> 01:08:09,547
[SPEAKER_01]: There was always a few.

1253
01:08:10,968 --> 01:08:12,569
[SPEAKER_03]: There was always a few, but...

1254
01:08:14,920 --> 01:08:18,203
[SPEAKER_03]: There wasn't as much as you might read about sometimes.

1255
01:08:18,943 --> 01:08:20,545
[SPEAKER_03]: Some of them overdid it.

1256
01:08:20,725 --> 01:08:23,827
[SPEAKER_03]: Ed Marshall, I suppose, I could have a picture.

1257
01:08:23,907 --> 01:08:24,608
[SPEAKER_03]: Big guy.

1258
01:08:25,268 --> 01:08:26,489
[SPEAKER_03]: And he liked his beer.

1259
01:08:26,869 --> 01:08:27,650
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, his liquor.

1260
01:08:27,770 --> 01:08:28,971
[SPEAKER_01]: He's the one that got killed?

1261
01:08:29,071 --> 01:08:29,752
[SPEAKER_03]: Got killed.

1262
01:08:30,132 --> 01:08:32,654
[SPEAKER_02]: Was that out of drinking?

1263
01:08:32,694 --> 01:08:39,419
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, he got killed when he was down home in Alabama before he came to spring training.

1264
01:08:40,220 --> 01:08:41,701
[SPEAKER_03]: They gave him a party or something.

1265
01:08:41,781 --> 01:08:42,702
[SPEAKER_03]: Ed could get mean.

1266
01:08:43,955 --> 01:08:45,897
[SPEAKER_03]: I think that might have been what happened to him.

1267
01:08:47,539 --> 01:08:53,644
[SPEAKER_03]: But he was a good old boy, but when he got a drink in him, boy, he was...

1268
01:08:55,066 --> 01:08:56,787
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a tough son of a gun.

1269
01:08:57,088 --> 01:09:01,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me ask you a couple of questions here.

1270
01:09:04,682 --> 01:09:09,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever he starts to say something to me or wants an answer, I'm surprised at the length of the cigarette.

1271
01:09:09,625 --> 01:09:10,125
[SPEAKER_00]: It's so cut out.

1272
01:09:10,306 --> 01:09:11,767
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't mean that.

1273
01:09:11,787 --> 01:09:13,828
[SPEAKER_03]: You've got to be careful what you say.

1274
01:09:13,908 --> 01:09:14,528
[SPEAKER_03]: I have another one.

1275
01:09:14,588 --> 01:09:15,489
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, you got it.

1276
01:09:15,629 --> 01:09:22,653
[SPEAKER_01]: I wanted to ask you how you like baseball.

1277
01:09:22,673 --> 01:09:24,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you go to the games very often?

1278
01:09:25,575 --> 01:09:29,097
[SPEAKER_02]: I went to games, especially the time that it meant with pitching.

1279
01:09:30,758 --> 01:09:32,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Did it make you nervous when he was pitching?

1280
01:09:32,699 --> 01:09:34,779
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, he was in a close game.

1281
01:09:35,860 --> 01:09:36,760
[SPEAKER_02]: You know what I mean.

1282
01:09:36,840 --> 01:09:41,962
[SPEAKER_02]: If he got by, say, the fifth inning and had a lead, I was pretty well satisfied.

1283
01:09:42,662 --> 01:09:46,663
[SPEAKER_01]: Did the wives sit together in the various teams he was with?

1284
01:09:49,784 --> 01:09:52,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you get to travel much with the team, or didn't wives travel?

1285
01:09:52,565 --> 01:09:56,667
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I came to spring training with him a couple of times, but the other times I stayed home.

1286
01:09:57,808 --> 01:09:59,770
[SPEAKER_01]: During the season, though, they couldn't travel with me.

1287
01:09:59,810 --> 01:10:10,519
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you could travel, but I took advantage of that with visiting with his parents and with my sister, see, in Boston and New York.

1288
01:10:12,120 --> 01:10:12,721
[SPEAKER_02]: Where are you going?

1289
01:10:15,663 --> 01:10:18,425
[SPEAKER_03]: Where are you going, sweetie?

1290
01:10:18,505 --> 01:10:19,066
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, uh...

1291
01:10:21,161 --> 01:10:24,262
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, now you have my life story.

1292
01:10:24,402 --> 01:10:29,304
[SPEAKER_01]: A couple more loose ends here.

1293
01:10:30,864 --> 01:10:34,565
[SPEAKER_01]: After that 34 season, were you released?

1294
01:10:35,166 --> 01:10:36,406
[SPEAKER_03]: Unconditional release.

1295
01:10:36,426 --> 01:10:39,287
[SPEAKER_03]: That was my Christmas present.

1296
01:10:39,647 --> 01:10:40,967
[SPEAKER_03]: I got it during December.

1297
01:10:41,067 --> 01:10:43,068
[SPEAKER_03]: How did they phrase it?

1298
01:10:43,628 --> 01:10:44,148
[SPEAKER_03]: That's all.

1299
01:10:44,509 --> 01:10:47,530
[SPEAKER_03]: There was a regular little form in those days.

1300
01:10:49,483 --> 01:10:50,683
[SPEAKER_03]: They had printing on there.

1301
01:10:50,703 --> 01:11:00,767
[SPEAKER_03]: I can't remember exactly, but your contract has been assigned to so-and-so or something else.

1302
01:11:00,827 --> 01:11:01,948
[SPEAKER_03]: I've forgotten just what.

1303
01:11:01,968 --> 01:11:06,269
[SPEAKER_03]: But they'd written across the front of it here, unconditionally released.

1304
01:11:08,510 --> 01:11:10,871
[SPEAKER_03]: So you're through, that's it.

1305
01:11:11,011 --> 01:11:14,612
[SPEAKER_01]: No further verbal communication at all?

1306
01:11:14,832 --> 01:11:17,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you have a hint this might be coming?

1307
01:11:19,364 --> 01:11:23,447
[SPEAKER_03]: I had expected it really before that because of the years I was having up there.

1308
01:11:23,527 --> 01:11:25,148
[SPEAKER_03]: I wasn't any help to them.

1309
01:11:26,429 --> 01:11:27,109
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know.

1310
01:11:29,010 --> 01:11:33,153
[SPEAKER_03]: But I figured I got in 11 years, so I had no kick.

1311
01:11:36,315 --> 01:11:41,098
[SPEAKER_01]: So what, in 35 then, you tried to catch on with the Reds, you said?

1312
01:11:41,178 --> 01:11:43,259
[SPEAKER_03]: 35, I tried to catch on with the Reds.

1313
01:11:45,501 --> 01:11:47,542
[SPEAKER_03]: And I had this bad arm, as I told you.

1314
01:11:48,688 --> 01:11:50,169
[SPEAKER_03]: I didn't do it any good over there.

1315
01:11:50,809 --> 01:11:51,689
[SPEAKER_03]: I hurt it worse.

1316
01:11:52,969 --> 01:11:57,671
[SPEAKER_03]: Then I went over to St. Petersburg where Bill McKinley was managing the Braves.

1317
01:11:59,351 --> 01:12:02,132
[SPEAKER_03]: And Bill was real, he was a nice guy.

1318
01:12:02,652 --> 01:12:03,773
[SPEAKER_03]: And he said, where have you been?

1319
01:12:03,853 --> 01:12:04,853
[SPEAKER_03]: I've been looking for you.

1320
01:12:04,873 --> 01:12:06,673
[SPEAKER_03]: I thought you were coming over first.

1321
01:12:06,693 --> 01:12:16,596
[SPEAKER_03]: So I told him, well, I tried out with Cincinnati because Danny McCraden was over there and I thought my wife and his wife being sisters would be together.

1322
01:12:19,058 --> 01:12:27,359
[SPEAKER_03]: Bill gave me a uniform, put me out on the mound there to throw the batting practice.

1323
01:12:27,379 --> 01:12:28,140
[SPEAKER_03]: And you could tell.

1324
01:12:29,240 --> 01:12:31,100
[SPEAKER_03]: And Frankie Frisch was there with him.

1325
01:12:32,060 --> 01:12:36,041
[SPEAKER_03]: And they were standing behind the cage when I was pitching, throwing batting practice.

1326
01:12:36,501 --> 01:12:38,121
[SPEAKER_03]: And I could see Frisch go like this.

1327
01:12:40,182 --> 01:12:40,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Shaking his head.

1328
01:12:41,102 --> 01:12:43,182
[SPEAKER_03]: I knew what that meant.

1329
01:12:44,002 --> 01:12:48,603
[SPEAKER_03]: So Bill told me, he went in the clubhouse with me and talked to me.

1330
01:12:49,223 --> 01:12:50,283
[SPEAKER_03]: He asked me if I could play.

1331
01:12:51,864 --> 01:12:53,305
[SPEAKER_03]: And he said, gee, it's a shame.

1332
01:12:53,345 --> 01:12:56,726
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, if you can do anything, get it fixed up.

1333
01:12:56,746 --> 01:12:57,946
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, you've got a job.

1334
01:12:58,787 --> 01:12:59,427
[SPEAKER_03]: And I couldn't.

1335
01:12:59,587 --> 01:13:00,227
[SPEAKER_03]: It was gone.

1336
01:13:00,247 --> 01:13:01,328
[SPEAKER_03]: That was all there was to it.

1337
01:13:02,208 --> 01:13:04,969
[SPEAKER_02]: I tried to get him to take a minor league managerial job.

1338
01:13:05,789 --> 01:13:09,491
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I tried to get a managing job, but you had to play at those days.

1339
01:13:10,051 --> 01:13:11,672
[SPEAKER_03]: I went up to the meeting in Atlanta.

1340
01:13:11,992 --> 01:13:17,194
[SPEAKER_03]: I wanted to manage over, and I had all kinds of minor league offers to pitch.

1341
01:13:17,983 --> 01:13:20,304
[SPEAKER_03]: But I couldn't pitch, so what was the sense?

1342
01:13:20,344 --> 01:13:23,745
[SPEAKER_03]: So I tried to get a managing job.

1343
01:13:24,665 --> 01:13:26,366
[SPEAKER_03]: But you had to pitch in those days.

1344
01:13:27,166 --> 01:13:37,129
[SPEAKER_03]: They had managers that were playing, not particularly pitchers, but playing at the same time.

1345
01:13:37,269 --> 01:13:39,550
[SPEAKER_03]: So I decided that was it.

1346
01:13:39,930 --> 01:13:40,751
[SPEAKER_03]: No, I didn't.

1347
01:13:41,511 --> 01:13:43,211
[SPEAKER_03]: I was going to be an umpire.

1348
01:13:44,972 --> 01:13:47,893
[SPEAKER_03]: And Ford Frick was the commissioner then.

1349
01:13:48,772 --> 01:13:54,915
[SPEAKER_03]: good friend of mine because he, when I first broke in with the Yankees, he was a newspaper man over in New York.

1350
01:13:55,996 --> 01:13:57,357
[SPEAKER_03]: And he and I were good friends.

1351
01:13:57,497 --> 01:14:06,362
[SPEAKER_03]: And he told me, he said, why don't you go to umpire, you know, he said, I'll keep an eye on you.

1352
01:14:06,382 --> 01:14:09,103
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's all you needed if he kept an eye on you.

1353
01:14:09,724 --> 01:14:11,344
[SPEAKER_03]: You only need that encouragement.

1354
01:14:12,895 --> 01:14:16,417
[SPEAKER_02]: I couldn't umpire.

1355
01:14:16,557 --> 01:14:20,738
[SPEAKER_03]: I tell you, I was the lousiest uptight you ever saw.

1356
01:14:21,439 --> 01:14:28,082
[SPEAKER_03]: I saw, I umpired a game between Cincinnati and St. Louis one time in spring training and they liked to kill me.

1357
01:14:29,582 --> 01:14:30,703
[SPEAKER_03]: I just couldn't do it.

1358
01:14:30,763 --> 01:14:32,083
[SPEAKER_03]: I just couldn't call it.

1359
01:14:33,204 --> 01:14:36,625
[SPEAKER_03]: So I decided that wasn't for me.

1360
01:14:37,726 --> 01:14:39,687
[SPEAKER_03]: So I said, I guess I better go to work.

1361
01:14:40,027 --> 01:14:40,547
[SPEAKER_03]: What did you do?

1362
01:14:42,118 --> 01:14:44,579
[SPEAKER_03]: When I first got out, I ran a filling station.

1363
01:14:44,659 --> 01:14:47,080
[SPEAKER_03]: I bought a filling station over in Tompkins Springs.

1364
01:14:48,021 --> 01:14:48,981
[SPEAKER_03]: I was going to get rich.

1365
01:14:50,842 --> 01:14:53,184
[SPEAKER_03]: I lost my shirt on then.

1366
01:14:53,224 --> 01:14:57,286
[SPEAKER_03]: I got out of there and I came to Tampa and managed the restaurant.

1367
01:14:59,046 --> 01:15:02,508
[SPEAKER_03]: And I quit that and then I went to the sheriff's office and I stayed.

1368
01:15:02,528 --> 01:15:04,169
[SPEAKER_03]: That's where I finally wound up.

1369
01:15:05,149 --> 01:15:06,050
[SPEAKER_03]: I was with them for about

1370
01:15:06,627 --> 01:15:07,667
[SPEAKER_03]: What's 16 years?

1371
01:15:07,788 --> 01:15:09,488
[SPEAKER_03]: Deputy Sheriff, police sheriff.

1372
01:15:10,289 --> 01:15:14,671
[SPEAKER_03]: I've got a rating on him besides his rating.

1373
01:15:14,991 --> 01:15:15,951
[SPEAKER_03]: In Bradenton here?

1374
01:15:15,991 --> 01:15:17,432
[SPEAKER_03]: In Hillsboro, up in Tampa.

1375
01:15:18,512 --> 01:15:22,114
[SPEAKER_03]: See, we lived in Tampa for 30 years, wasn't it?

1376
01:15:22,134 --> 01:15:23,855
[SPEAKER_02]: From 41.

1377
01:15:23,995 --> 01:15:24,975
[SPEAKER_03]: 41 to 77.

1378
01:15:25,115 --> 01:15:26,736
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, over 30 years.

1379
01:15:28,597 --> 01:15:30,117
[SPEAKER_01]: You were from St. Louis though, then?

1380
01:15:30,137 --> 01:15:30,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I was.

1381
01:15:30,538 --> 01:15:35,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have any Danny McPhee stories?

1382
01:15:39,005 --> 01:15:40,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Danny was a good pitcher.

1383
01:15:40,245 --> 01:15:44,348
[SPEAKER_02]: Was he the coach up there?

1384
01:15:44,448 --> 01:15:46,469
[SPEAKER_02]: He coached up there for 20 years.

1385
01:16:06,790 --> 01:16:09,152
[SPEAKER_00]: It's pretty up there.

1386
01:16:10,452 --> 01:16:11,113
[SPEAKER_00]: I love it.

1387
01:16:12,153 --> 01:16:15,095
[SPEAKER_03]: I love it in the summertime.

1388
01:16:15,356 --> 01:16:21,039
[SPEAKER_02]: It's beautiful in the summer, but boy, it gets too cold for me.

1389
01:16:21,600 --> 01:16:25,002
[SPEAKER_03]: I have two 88-year-old cousins living up there and me.

1390
01:16:25,642 --> 01:16:26,583
[SPEAKER_03]: You're around.

1391
01:16:26,923 --> 01:16:29,104
[SPEAKER_03]: Some of those people you couldn't drag out of me.

1392
01:16:29,184 --> 01:16:29,825
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, Danny,

1393
01:16:31,015 --> 01:16:33,136
[SPEAKER_03]: Gee, not Florida for him.

1394
01:16:33,156 --> 01:16:34,416
[SPEAKER_03]: No, South.

1395
01:16:34,436 --> 01:16:36,577
[SPEAKER_03]: You couldn't get him out of that snow country.

1396
01:16:37,497 --> 01:16:40,898
[SPEAKER_00]: I think they did give up their hospital for the winter.

1397
01:16:40,918 --> 01:16:41,719
[SPEAKER_00]: They moved in.

1398
01:16:41,739 --> 01:16:43,459
[SPEAKER_00]: They usually moved in.

1399
01:16:43,559 --> 01:16:46,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Last year, they moved in to a hotel in Portland.

1400
01:16:46,660 --> 01:16:49,741
[SPEAKER_00]: This year, they moved in someplace else.

1401
01:16:49,881 --> 01:16:50,422
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

1402
01:16:50,442 --> 01:16:55,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's the original family homestead from the up in year one or something.

1403
01:16:56,064 --> 01:16:57,184
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, he wasn't there.

1404
01:16:58,095 --> 01:17:04,843
[SPEAKER_03]: Portland, New Brunswick, that was only about 30 miles north of Portland.

1405
01:17:04,883 --> 01:17:07,226
[SPEAKER_03]: He had a good job over there, Bowdoin College.

1406
01:17:10,385 --> 01:17:13,667
[SPEAKER_01]: played in the years you did it, it was about the train travel.

1407
01:17:14,107 --> 01:17:14,227
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh.

1408
01:17:14,347 --> 01:17:19,990
[SPEAKER_01]: And some of those rides from Boston to St. Louis, or from any place to St. Louis in the heat of the summer.

1409
01:17:20,110 --> 01:17:21,331
[SPEAKER_03]: It was a pain in the neck.

1410
01:17:21,351 --> 01:17:31,617
[SPEAKER_03]: You didn't have air conditioning, and you'd have your window open, and you know, when you're in your berth, and the old coal burners would throw those cinders back at you.

1411
01:17:31,897 --> 01:17:33,378
[SPEAKER_01]: You didn't fly by jet, did you?

1412
01:17:33,438 --> 01:17:35,439
[SPEAKER_03]: No, we didn't fly at all.

1413
01:17:35,899 --> 01:17:36,980
[SPEAKER_03]: Everything was by train.

1414
01:17:37,480 --> 01:17:39,261
[SPEAKER_03]: One trip we made from Detroit to

1415
01:17:39,981 --> 01:17:41,962
[SPEAKER_03]: But Cleveland, and vice versa, by boat.

1416
01:17:42,162 --> 01:17:43,783
[SPEAKER_03]: We'd go on a boat.

1417
01:17:43,864 --> 01:17:44,484
[SPEAKER_03]: That was nice.

1418
01:17:44,504 --> 01:17:45,164
[SPEAKER_03]: We liked it.

1419
01:17:45,485 --> 01:17:46,445
[SPEAKER_03]: I wonder why they did that.

1420
01:17:46,685 --> 01:17:47,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Just as a novelty?

1421
